But wait — if the raw client doesn't read at all, the server's send buffer fills after ~ tens of KB (kernel buffers). The 320KB message: sendData fragment 0 → sendOnSocket writes maybe 64KB+ then EWOULDBLOCK... Actually for _blockingSend (server), sendFragment→sendOnSocket non-blocking attempt, then... hmm wait, in the fragmented loop, sendFragment returns sendOnSocket() result — which returns true even on EWOULDBLOCK (breaks loop, returns true). So fragment loop continues appending all fragments to _txbuf until callback cancels at fragment 0. Then close → nested sendData(CLOSE) → _blockingSend flush → blocks up to 300ms (deadline) since client never reads → break → return. Close event fires on poll thread after deadline. bufferedAmount: closeSocket clears → 0. 

The raw client should read the handshake response but then stop. Note: if the raw client never reads, TCP backpressure happens quickly — good, this also exercises "对端不回复时...不能一直停在Closing".

